# Design: Migration runner and durable SQLite configuration

## Problem
The bootstrap shipped 001_initial.sql as plain DDL applied by tests.
Nothing recorded which schema versions a database had, nothing
prevented rewriting applied history, and durability pragmas lived in
migration SQL where SQLite silently ignores parts of them.

## Approach
Versioned, embedded, checksummed migrations applied one-per-transaction
by Go code on a single writer connection. The runner, not the SQL,
owns schema_migrations. Connection settings moved from 001_initial.sql
into DSN parameters applied to every pooled connection.

## Key Trade-offs
- One transaction per migration (not one for the whole set): a failed
  file rolls back alone and the next run resumes at the first pending
  version, which also makes "fix an unapplied broken file" legal while
  editing an applied one stays an error.
- Pragmas in code, not SQL: PRAGMA journal_mode cannot run inside a
  transaction and PRAGMA foreign_keys is a no-op there, so migration
  files must stay schema/data only.
- Readers use mode=ro pools with foreign_keys: read-only connections
  cannot create the database or recover the WAL, so the writer must be
  open first; that ordering is documented on DB.Reader.
- Version gaps in the file set are allowed (order is what the runner
  enforces), but back-filling a number below the newest applied
  version is rejected, keeping the set append-only.

## Gotchas
- count(*) on an external-content FTS5 table reads the content table,
  so index-vs-table consistency must be asserted with the
  integrity-check command and MATCH enumeration, not row counts.

